Z||000000||Joe4x4||02-21-2001||12:51 AM||||I came back from NJ inspection FAILED.<BR>I think my timing is off, I read past posts<BR>and read Haynes manuale .<BR>1) ground the a+b ( between the seat`s)<BR>2) hock up the timing light to #1 get the reading<BR>3) hock up #4 get reading <BR>4) divide the 2 <BR>5) that will be the # degree to set it on # 1 cyl?....is this correct <P>It is loseing power ,..I`m gonna replace plugs  and fuel filter,..wires,rotor, cap are new (1 year old ).<BR> <BR>Ok this what I know,.....what else can I do and check .<P>------------------<BR>Fiero got fire<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/exp.gif">          <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/animated_cat.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phil||02-21-2001||07:01 AM||||Not quite<BR>You want the ave. to be 10* btdc(at least on the V6), so take #1 and add it to #2 then divide by 2 and the ans. should be 10||||reg||1
Z||000002||862M4inCA||02-21-2001||06:37 PM||||To time the 86 4-cyl correctly you need to warm the engine to operating temperature. Then shut it all down, and ground the diagnostic terminal (located behind the plastic cigarette lighter cover in the center console), I use a paperclip. Start the engine back up and with the timing light connected to the #1 plug check the timing marks, record that reading. Now do the same for the #4 plug. Take the two numbers and divide to get the average. This number should be 8, if not you will need to turn the distributor and recheck #1 and 4 until you do get a average of 8 degrees. Remember to tighten the distributor down after your done. It would also probably be a good idea to shut the engine off before removing the paperclip. ||||reg||1
